  "summary": "A traditional Mexican pottery style with Spanish and indigenous influences.",
  "description": "Characterized by intricate hand-painted designs in cobalt blue, white, and yellow on a tin-glazed ceramic background, reflecting the fusion of European techniques and local craftsmanship. Developed in Puebla following the Spanish conquest, incorporating indigenous techniques and designs brought by artisans from Talavera de la Reina, Spain.",
  "culturalContext": "Talavera poblana — Puebla's regulated majolica (denominación de origen since 1997) — descends from Talavera de la Reina potters and Chinese export plates via the Manila galleon: blue-on-white hieroglyphic imitations, polychrome fauna (urupo \"half-world\" creatures), and church domes tiled entirely in the ware (San Francisco Acatepec). Strict glaze, clay and firing rules make genuine Talavera a legal category; the blue-and-orange palette signs Puebla's kitchens, facades and urinals (Museum of Talavera) alike.",
